#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106822} :activity/id #uuid "91efd670-1647-54ae-8540-d95ee4791ec7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050104 :topic/id #uuid "2d9b53c7-d4e4-5c8d-ad01-c539428002fb" :topic/math-academy-id 1401} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106822 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107127 :tutorial/content "When $C$ is a closed curve, there is always a well-defined notion of \"inside\" and \"outside\" $C.$ It is usually easy to determine which region is inside or outside an arbitrary closed curve using our natural intuition.\n\nOne important quantity that we often need when dealing with closed, oriented curves is the **outward-pointing unit normal vector,** usually denoted $\\mathbf n$.\n\nAs the name suggests:\n\n- $\\mathbf n$ is normal (i.e., perpendicular) to the curve $C$ at *every* point,\n\n- it is a unit vector (i.e., its magnitude equals $1$), and\n\n- it always points in the outward direction.\n\nSince $\\mathbf n$ must be normal to $C$ at *every* point, it must vary as we move around $C.$ Therefore, it is usually written either as a function of the parameter space (i.e., $\\mathbf n(t)$) or as a function of the coordinates of the points on $C$ (i.e., $\\mathbf n(x,y)$). \n\nAs a concrete example, let's consider the closed, positively oriented ellipse $C,$ parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = 3\\cos t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 2\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad t \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\n![](images/fe/fea4c20453e9c80493f5c5431859fd7918d75cbaa494ad0b3deeccacfae24019.png)\n\nFor convenience, we first express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n![](images/c7/c7d514dbfdb746d94487a7ebca6b239173b854010eeccb80b4afc06cd09a9c0e.svg)\n\nLet's calculate the vector $\\mathbf n$ for this curve. To do this, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA typical tangent vector for our curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/10/10ae867f682158bce2098234210206d4d7eb811be780505ca1984fb9e9516a27.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n![](images/27/27e11f86245d4331e33cec34742a7b6c395ff22fe95bcf465ec569f7168b4c0f.svg)\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get the vector $\\tilde{\\mathbf n}(t),$ given by\n\n![](images/00/006458079641036b283371fb36a94b39b11dcb09d796175a555539287218d8a7.svg)\n\nThe vector $\\tilde{\\mathbf n}(t)$ is shown below:\n\n![](images/7c/7ca9a18d78783a923b63fcc41a8bc41ccab5c4a12bb00f5c305e35e2e1bc40e5.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nThe vector $\\tilde{\\mathbf n}(t)$ in an outward-pointing normal vector, but it is not a unit vector. Therefore, we need to normalize it.\n\nNotice that  \n\n$$\n||\\mathbf r'(t)|| = \\sqrt{(3\\sin t)^2 + (2\\cos t)^2} = \\sqrt{9\\sin^2 t + 4\\cos^2 t}.\n$$\n\nFurthermore, the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore, we can calculate our outward-pointing unit vector $\\mathbf n$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}(t)}{||{r}^{′}(t)||} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the vector\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\dfrac{2\\cos t\\,\\mathbf i + 3\\sin t\\,\\mathbf j}{\\sqrt{9\\sin^2 t + 4\\cos^2 t}}\n$$\n\ngives us an outward-pointing unit normal vector to every point on our ellipse." :tutorial/id #uuid "f96a7b70-f713-5ac8-afbe-0bc9a8caf7b8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5850 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "76d73a7c-6865-5f65-9779-6beec167812a" :step/math-academy-id 35824 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106823}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106823 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105855 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105691 :question/id #uuid "4b8266ba-b293-541f-9e4f-5005a6184816" :question/math-academy-id "e-9566"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050722}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262669} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522808}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Positively Oriented Curve"} :step/id #uuid "5c455c39-68b2-555e-aa74-5eb5be9fa04a" :step/math-academy-id 35838 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106825}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106824 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105856 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105692 :question/id #uuid "ad8da3b7-7e57-58bb-a0d2-8cdbddf80239" :question/math-academy-id "e-9571"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522803}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Negatively Oriented Curve"} :step/id #uuid "c850f239-ce6c-5c3e-8ee3-0ed3c4d28237" :step/math-academy-id 35855 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106826}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106825 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107128 :tutorial/content "When curves are *negatively* oriented, the procedure of finding the outward-pointing unit normal vector is almost the same. \n\nThe only difference is the second step. If $C$ is negatively oriented (i.e., oriented *clockwise*), we must rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\n![](images/76/7641dfd05a69d6ffa2d0619d1de4a8d3bb6ec63b479b929d0f8e52bf910c9448.png)\n\nLet's take a look at an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "997b1386-6f87-5686-9745-a45b89423d14" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5861 :tutorial/title "Case of Negatively Oriented Curves"} :step/id #uuid "ec49a1fe-8e0d-5f91-a696-e05afdb8eeab" :step/math-academy-id 35876 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106824}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106826 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107129 :tutorial/content "Let's consider a general, closed curve $C$ in the plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = x(t)\\,\\mathbf{i} + y(t)\\,\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nWe will assume that $C$ is oriented counterclockwise. Our goal is to derive a general formula for $\\mathbf n.$ \n\nAs always, we first express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n![](images/48/48517c1b1d9915c8f739f8a3979778eb7ea8ddebe01f276034f8df1c301ce802.svg)\n\nTo calculate $\\mathbf n,$ we follow our usual three steps.\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n![](images/49/492e23c319bc0681cf1c65d0ff91fccdde02686891b3ae1c1daf9f512f38a7e7.svg)\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get the vector \n![](images/e4/e44acf1d3bdb1caaf25b89faa191a58faa7979881d9a7093ec23ea9ab8405103.svg)\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNotice that  \n\n$$\n||\\mathbf r'(t)|| = \\sqrt{(x'(t))^2 + (y'(t))^2}.\n$$\n\nFurthermore, the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore, we can calculate our outward-pointing unit vector $\\mathbf n$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) \\\\ -{x}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\,i-\\frac{{x}^{′}(t)}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)\\,i-{x}^{′}(t)\\,j}{\\sqrt{({x}^{′}(t))^{2}+({y}^{′}(t))^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d4a8dc89-6fbc-58b6-af1b-92bb93b085ec" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5864 :tutorial/title "Deriving the General Formula for a Positively Oriented Curve"} :step/id #uuid "369de3f2-63c7-51db-879c-c7cb6e22ec92" :step/math-academy-id 35879}] :activity/title "Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vectors in 2D" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186106019}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445295} :activity/id #uuid "be844a0b-1b21-5bf9-af56-f92614fa7bb7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050104 :topic/id #uuid "2d9b53c7-d4e4-5c8d-ad01-c539428002fb" :topic/math-academy-id 1401} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445294 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07f0b6be-b8a6-509c-9eef-fddd1e4c52cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445263} {:answer/id #uuid "4e0f7628-1647-5244-8346-f685935b3b5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445269} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c95c20-8a83-5b7f-a94b-48be5207dcd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445278} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f1e04e-c989-540c-945e-5530f0a9ef5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445279} {:answer/id #uuid "ebba1cbb-de04-5f96-928a-2186ab923ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445285}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445278} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f82b12c-6ce8-5ff6-b513-2e1efd6f0437" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7df57c40-4642-538c-b446-11650ad2af53" :question/math-academy-id "q-126916" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"} :step/id #uuid "01d78fae-df26-523a-a169-023a86387db3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445297}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445295 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106040 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0acdd021-7840-54cb-948b-ea5f4720bcd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445264} {:answer/id #uuid "554fb461-f8fc-5f02-8c53-5d662d712545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445270} {:answer/id #uuid "7c37312e-1d93-53a0-ba9c-7e273739a5c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445274} {:answer/id #uuid "acdafd0f-04a3-50f3-9016-229636fea9cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445277} {:answer/id #uuid "e4176979-2302-53d4-9836-168bdc17d4a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445283}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445277} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cfca16d-0fcb-5667-80fc-4f868f06662a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6da7c1a3-2c67-58ef-8929-9aec32acf858" :question/math-academy-id "q-126823" :question/problem "The closed, positively oriented curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"} :step/id #uuid "091b59dd-5e66-5d54-a04a-5e006f7a4412" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445294}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445296 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445291 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12c2611f-f075-5bef-9b12-8e6d7c6016ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445265} {:answer/id #uuid "21912693-46e5-54c5-885b-ffece0e4f4ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445266} {:answer/id #uuid "322819d7-8ec8-5e47-8eb6-5ed5edaa65a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445268} {:answer/id #uuid "b73f5019-9bf1-59b9-bbe0-c37feaebc1f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445280} {:answer/id #uuid "d0954837-8233-5864-88b9-32dce2d50d2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445281}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445280} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee2b7e94-139d-52df-a733-a979d04555a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "78d41512-3eb5-5609-aad8-a61279c74c85" :question/math-academy-id "q-149681" :question/problem "The closed, positively oriented curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"} :step/id #uuid "792a52ce-5964-52dd-90b1-8b9d723859c6"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445297 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106043 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bde73f4-acbe-5a30-bd8d-32583815fc67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445272} {:answer/id #uuid "8a98e20c-8619-5648-a5cf-3ffe0e3b9b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445276} {:answer/id #uuid "da1537f7-60f2-5487-98fb-d8eb37445eb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445282} {:answer/id #uuid "e881b58b-f16e-577e-bb7a-de715dbd1a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445284} {:answer/id #uuid "fd95b973-cef8-59e2-935c-7fcafbf777f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :an