ned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"} :step/id #uuid "80749284-e241-5549-b174-88307b7b6777"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522888 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522782 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2881a3fe-3c53-5f50-b217-d4f72fd2ff75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522112} {:answer/id #uuid "c0c3f5a8-5b5d-5fc0-b695-ef9b93941451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522418} {:answer/id #uuid "c6b1c6e9-c74e-5f71-ac2e-f8c7b792b2f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522436} {:answer/id #