se rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"} :step/id #uuid "91c89fa2-6786-592c-acc2-2a4b9d5baaa5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536030}}] :activity/title "Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vectors in 2D" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535427}]}
